  "raw": {
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Professional-Writing_JR10015888": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Professional Writing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor of Professional Writing Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Position Summary: The Department of Humanities at the University of Wisconsin-Platteville invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Professional Writing , beginning August 2027. Responsibilities for this position include teaching undergraduate courses, research and scholarly activity, and university and departmental service. This is a full-time benefits-eligible position with a salary range of $58,000 - $62,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience. The successful candidate will join a vibrant program that offers majors in Literature, Media, and Culture; Professional Writing; and English Education. We seek a candidate who will contribute to and help shape the future of the English program’s Professional Writing major, including reviewing and revising the Professional Writing curriculum to ensure that it reflects current disciplinary practices, emerging technologies, and student interests. We especially welcome candidates interested in developing innovative and experiential learning opportunities that connect students’ work to professional contexts and community or industry partners. We seek a teacher-scholar whose work focuses on writing and communication in professional, technical, scientific, or other applied contexts. We are particularly interested in candidates whose teaching and scholarship can connect professional writing with one or more areas of strength at UW-Platteville: the sciences, agriculture, engineering, healthcare, and related fields. Relevant areas of expertise might include, but are not limited to, technical and professional communication; science, health, environmental, or agricultural communication; digital and multimodal writing; usability and user experience; workplace writing; rhetoric of science and technology; and AI-mediated communication. Key Job Responsibilities: The successful candidate will: Teach undergraduate courses in professional and technical writing and related areas. Depending on expertise and curricular needs, courses may include Technical Writing, Writing for Science and Research, Designing for Usability, Writing, Editing, and Publishing in Multiple Media, rhetoric, and additional courses in the candidate’s area(s) of specialization. Collaborate with English faculty to review, revise, and strengthen the Professional Writing major, including developing new courses and curricular opportunities responsive to changes in professional communication and emerging technologies. Develop interdisciplinary connections with programs across the university, particularly in areas such as engineering, science, agriculture, and health-related fields. Create opportunities for students to apply writing, editing, research, design, and communication skills through experiential learning, which may include client-based projects, internships, publishing, community partnerships, or other forms of applied work. Maintain an active program of scholarship and/or creative or professional activity appropriate to the candidate’s field. Advise and mentor students in the Professional Writing program. Contribute to first-year and/or general education writing instruction as appropriate. Participate in department, college, university, and professional service. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Rhetoric and Composition, Writing Studies, Technical and Professional Communication, English, or a closely related field earned by January 1, 2028. Minimum of one semester of teaching professional, technical, scientific, workplace, or other forms of applied writing and communication. Minimum of one semester of undergraduate teaching experience. Evidence of scholarly and professional activity, such as publications, presentations, or research projects. Excellent written and oral communication skills. To be considered for this position, applicants must respond to all required qualifications . Why it is great to be a Pioneer: The University of Wisconsin-Platteville, founded in 1866, offers associate, baccalaureate, and master’s degree programs in a broad spectrum of disciplines including: science, technology, engineering, and mathematics; criminal justice; education; business; agriculture; and liberal arts. The Platteville campus is located in Southwest Wisconsin's largest community, which has a rich history rooted in mining —particularly lead mining —dating back to the early 19th century. The region offers excellent school systems, high-quality medical and hospital facilities, outstanding recreational opportunities, and vibrant businesses and industries. UW-Platteville serves as a cultural and educational center for the Tri-State region of Illinois, Iowa, and Wisconsin. The English program at the University of Wisconsin-Platteville is a part of a large, vibrant humanities program, with strengths in many areas, including professional writing, creative writing, film, literature, and education. Each year, the department sponsors a World Languages Day, visiting writer and scholar events, and a launch party for the annual issue of Spirit Lake Review, a nationally published literary journal run by our undergraduate students. Application Deadline: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by November 14, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position has been filled. The following documents are required for applicant consideration: Letter of application addressing all required qualifications. A current curriculum vitae. Contact information (name, telephone number, and email address) for three professional references . Pedagogy Statement that reflects the candidate’s perspective on the integration of teaching, research, and scholarly work to create transformative student learning experiences. Additional documents as needed. Legal Notices and Important Information:  Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you and your references to answer questions regarding sexual violence and sexual harassment.   The University ensures physical accessibility to work environments for persons with disabilities and will provided reasonable accommodations to ensure equal access to employment. Upon request, the University will provide reasonable accommodations for religious observances and practices.   The University is committed to a program of affirmative action for women, racial minorities, persons with disabilities, disabled veterans and veterans of the Vietnam era. While the Chancellor assumes overall responsibility for the success of the program, university administrators and supervisors are responsible and accountable for implementation. Authority for monitoring the program is delegated to Human Resources.   Each individual associated with the University is called upon to pledge a new and revitalized commitment to build and maintain a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination, an environment that fosters mutual respect, recognizes the dignity and worth of all people, and promotes to the fullest, equal employment opportunity through affirmative action.   In compliance with the Clery Act of 1998, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville  Annual Security & Annual Fire Safety Report  is available for review. Call the UW-Platteville Campus Police Office at 608.342.1584 for a paper copy of the annual report. The Universities of Wisconsin will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released.  See Wis. Stat. §. 19.36(7). UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. Required Postings: Labor Law Poster - English Labor Law Poster - Spanish Families First Coronavirus Response Act Update"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Chemistry_JR10015757": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor of Chemistry Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Position Summary: The Department of Chemistry at the University of Wisconsin-Platteville invites applications for a tenure-track position in Analytical or Physical Chemistry.  This a full-time, nine-month, benefits eligible position starting in August 2027.  There may be an opportunity to begin in January 2027. This position is a faculty position with responsibilities for teaching a full faculty load in Chemistry.  The successful candidate is expected to build an undergraduate research program and participate actively in service to the Department, College of EMS, and University.  Teaching assignments are expected to come from a pool of general chemistries, and upper-level courses in analytical and possibly physical chemistry.  Supporting the Chemistry Department initiatives and programs related to forensic science applications is expected.  Key Job Responsibilities: Teaching responsibilities include lecture and lab courses in general chemistry, and upper-level courses in analytical chemistry. Curriculum development in analytical chemistry is expected. The successful candidate is expected to establish an independent research program that involves undergraduate students. Additional responsibilities include seeking external funding for undergraduate research, contributing to Departmental emphases related to forensic science applications and programming, assisting with instrument maintenance, academic advising, performing committee service and other duties as assigned. Chemistry Department The Chemistry Department currently consists of 9 tenured/tenure-track faculty, 3 instructional academic staff, and 2 lab technicians. The Department offers BS degrees in Chemistry that include program emphases certified by the American Chemical Society (ACS). Available degree emphases include a standard track, Biochemistry, Criminalistics, and Criminalistics with a DNA focus. The Criminalistics Emphases are unique undergraduate programs in the State. The Department also offers a Chemistry Minor and a 2-year Pre-Pharmacy Program. The Department has approximately 80 majors and graduates 12 to 15 students per year. Faculty within the program work closely with students and provide several opportunities for undergraduate research. For more information, please see the departmental web page at: https://www.uwplatt.edu/department/chemistry. College of Engineering, Mathematics, and Science The College of EMS serves 2,653 undergraduate students and 73 graduate students. The College of EMS values being a key player in our undergraduate students’ success through teaching excellence, continuously improving our teaching skills, creating engaging learning environments, and implementing known best practices in STEM education, nurturing a culture of care toward our students and our colleagues, serving students with a wide range of preparation and meeting our students where they are, preparing our students for the rigors of the STEM workplace through high standards, and providing job-ready skills to our students through authentic, applied, and hands-on learning experiences. Application Deadline: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by November 7, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position has been filled. The following documents are required for applicant consideration: A letter of application addressing all required qualifications. A curriculum vitae or resume. A separate statement of teaching philosophy and experience (2 page limit). A separate statement of research plans that includes projects to engage undergraduate students (3 page limit). Copies of graduate and undergraduate transcripts (unofficial copies are acceptable). Contact information (name, telephone number, and email address) for three professional references (at least one of whom can speak to teaching ability). Legal Notices and Important Information:  Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you and your references to answer questions regarding sexual violence and sexual harassment.   The University ensures physical accessibility to work environments for persons with disabilities and will provided reasonable accommodations to ensure equal access to employment. Upon request, the University will provide reasonable accommodations for religious observances and practices.   The University is committed to a program of affirmative action for women, racial minorities, persons with disabilities, disabled veterans and veterans of the Vietnam era. While the Chancellor assumes overall responsibility for the success of the program, university administrators and supervisors are responsible and accountable for implementation. Authority for monitoring the program is delegated to Human Resources.   Each individual associated with the University is called upon to pledge a new and revitalized commitment to build and maintain a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination, an environment that fosters mutual respect, recognizes the dignity and worth of all people, and promotes to the fullest, equal employment opportunity through affirmative action.   In compliance with the Clery Act of 1998, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville  Annual Security & Annual Fire Safety Report  is available for review. Call the UW-Platteville Campus Police Office at 608.342.1584 for a paper copy of the annual report. The Universities of Wisconsin will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released.  See Wis. Stat. §. 19.36(7). UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. Required Postings: Labor Law Poster - English Labor Law Poster - Spanish Families First Coronavirus Response Act Update"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Lecturer-of-Voice_JR10015744": {
      "title": "Lecturer of Voice",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Lecturer of Voice Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Terminal (Fixed Term) Job Profile: Lecturer Position Summary: The Performing and Visual Arts Department at the University of Wisconsin–Platteville is seeking an outstanding vocal performer and educator to join our music program as a Lecturer of Voice , beginning August 2027. This is a full-time, nine-month, benefits-eligible position with a salary range of $40,000 - $43,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience. We seek a collaborative colleague who will inspire students, contribute to a vibrant and supportive learning environment, and actively engage with students, faculty, and the university community. Key Job Responsibilities: Teach applied voice to music majors, music minors, and non-degree music students, with responsibility for preparing music majors through senior recital. Teach courses such as Diction for Singers I and Diction for Singers II, Convocation, Music Appreciation, and other courses based on departmental needs and the candidate’s areas of expertise. Participate in weekly Voice Convocation and voice-area evaluations, including voice hearings, juries, and Upper Division Examinations. Work closely with the Vocal Area Coordinator and other music faculty to support the vocal area and music program. Recruit and retain voice students and contribute to the growth of the voice area. Collaborate and perform with faculty and university ensembles as appropriate. Participate in departmental concerts, recruitment events, festivals, and other program activities. Maintain an active professional profile as a performer and teacher in the field of voice. Participate in departmental and university service as appropriate. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in Music with an emphasis in Voice, Vocal Performance, Vocal Pedagogy, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience teaching applied voice at the college or university level. Demonstrated experience at high artistic level as a vocal performer. Ability to recruit, mentor, and retain voice students. Strong commitment to undergraduate education and collaboration with students and faculty. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Why it is great to be a Pioneer: The University of Wisconsin-Platteville, founded in 1866, offers associate, baccalaureate, and master’s degree programs in a broad spectrum of disciplines including: science, technology, engineering, and mathematics; criminal justice; education; business; agriculture; and liberal arts. The Platteville campus is located in Southwest Wisconsin's largest community, which has a rich history rooted in mining —particularly lead mining —dating back to the early 19th century. The region offers excellent school systems, high-quality medical and hospital facilities, outstanding recreational opportunities, and vibrant businesses and industries. UW-Platteville serves as a cultural and educational center for the Tri-State region of Illinois, Iowa, and Wisconsin. Application Deadline: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by November 7, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position has been filled. The following documents are required for applicant consideration: Cover letter addressing qualifications and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae. One representative performance recording demonstrating the candidate’s vocal performance (link preferred). Additional recordings may be requested from candidates who advance in the search. Contact information for three professional references. For questions regarding this position, please contact the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Kaju Lee, at leekaju@uwplatt.edu. Legal Notices and Important Information:  Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you and your references to answer questions regarding sexual violence and sexual harassment.   The University ensures physical accessibility to work environments for persons with disabilities and will provided reasonable accommodations to ensure equal access to employment. Upon request, the University will provide reasonable accommodations for religious observances and practices.   The University is committed to a program of affirmative action for women, racial minorities, persons with disabilities, disabled veterans and veterans of the Vietnam era. While the Chancellor assumes overall responsibility for the success of the program, university administrators and supervisors are responsible and accountable for implementation. Authority for monitoring the program is delegated to Human Resources.   Each individual associated with the University is called upon to pledge a new and revitalized commitment to build and maintain a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination, an environment that fosters mutual respect, recognizes the dignity and worth of all people, and promotes to the fullest, equal employment opportunity through affirmative action.   In compliance with the Clery Act of 1998, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville  Annual Security & Annual Fire Safety Report  is available for review. Call the UW-Platteville Campus Police Office at 608.342.1584 for a paper copy of the annual report. The Universities of Wisconsin will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released.  See Wis. Stat. §. 19.36(7). UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. Required Postings: Labor Law Poster - English Labor Law Poster - Spanish Families First Coronavirus Response Act Update"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Music-Director-of-Choral-Studies_JR10015746-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Music/Director of Choral Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor of Music/Director of Choral Studies Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Position Summary: The Performing and Visual Arts Department at the University of Wisconsin-Platteville invites applications for a collaborative and community-minded colleague to serve as Director of Choral Studies at the rank of Assistant Professor , beginning in August 2027. This is a full-time, tenure-track, benefits-eligible position with a salary range of $60,000 - $68,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience. The choral department at UW-Platteville has a long and rich tradition of excellence in performance and in the training of undergraduate choral conductors and music educators. UW-Platteville undergraduates have held significant positions in all levels of choral music, houses of worship, and communities in Wisconsin and beyond. The successful candidate will contribute to the continued success and growth of the choral and music programs while building on the program’s tradition of excellence and responding to current trends in the profession. The candidate will collaborate with the Associate Director of Choirs and other music faculty to support the undergraduate program, its ensembles, students, and activities. Key Job Responsibilities: Lead UW-Platteville’s choral ensembles. Teach undergraduate choral literature, choral conducting, music history, and other courses based on departmental needs and the candidate’s areas of expertise. Work closely with other music faculty to support the music program. Recruit and retain students and contribute to the growth of the music program and greater Platteville community. Collaborate with faculty and university ensembles as appropriate. Effectively create and administer departmental concerts, recruitment events, festivals, and other program activities. Maintain an active professional profile as a performer and teacher in the field of choral music. Participate in departmental and university service as appropriate. Required Qualifications: Doctoral degree in Choral Conducting, Choral Music Education, Music Education, Music, or a closely related field, conferred by the position start date. 1 semester (4.5 months) of teaching and conducting at the collegiate level. Conducting experience must include members with varying skill levels and background. 1 semester (4.5 months) of collaborating with schools, community, or professional organizations. Professional engagement with at least one national or international choral organization. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Why it is great to be a Pioneer: The University of Wisconsin-Platteville, founded in 1866, offers associate, baccalaureate, and master’s degree programs in a broad spectrum of disciplines including: science, technology, engineering, and mathematics; criminal justice; education; business; agriculture; and liberal arts. The Platteville campus is located in Southwest Wisconsin's largest community, which has a rich history rooted in mining —particularly lead mining —dating back to the early 19th century. The region offers excellent school systems, high-quality medical and hospital facilities, outstanding recreational opportunities, and vibrant businesses and industries. UW-Platteville serves as a cultural and educational center for the Tri-State region of Illinois, Iowa, and Wisconsin. About the Music Program An accredited member of the National Association of Schools of Music, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville serves over 500 students from across the university who study in our classes, perform in our ensembles, or engage in our instrumental and vocal studios. In addition, the Music program serves as a vital cultural resource in our region, annually hosting a choral festival, a jazz festival, a piano clinic, an honors band, and the state-level solo and ensemble competition that draw high school music students and teachers from southwest Wisconsin and the surrounding tri-state area. The Choral program includes five vibrant ensembles, four full-time faculty members, a myriad of practicum-based opportunities for undergraduate students, and a wealth of rewarding experiences for those who love to sing and want to be a part of our choral community. Each choir is comprised of students pursuing a diverse spectrum of majors, and all singers are welcome, regardless of prior experience or ability. Application deadline: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by November 7, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position has been filled. The following documents are required for applicant consideration: A letter of interest. Current curriculum vitae. Names and contact information for three professional references. Statement of conducting and teaching philosophy. Links to two videos - one of a choral rehearsal and one of a live choral performance, maximum 20 minutes in length for each (with the camera facing you from the ensemble’s perspective). For questions regarding this position, please contact Dr. Elise DesChamps at deschampse@uwplatt.edu. Legal Notices and Important Information:  Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you and your references to answer questions regarding sexual violence and sexual harassment.   The University ensures physical accessibility to work environments for persons with disabilities and will provided reasonable accommodations to ensure equal access to employment. Upon request, the University will provide reasonable accommodations for religious observances and practices.   The University is committed to a program of affirmative action for women, racial minorities, persons with disabilities, disabled veterans and veterans of the Vietnam era. While the Chancellor assumes overall responsibility for the success of the program, university administrators and supervisors are responsible and accountable for implementation. Authority for monitoring the program is delegated to Human Resources.   Each individual associated with the University is called upon to pledge a new and revitalized commitment to build and maintain a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination, an environment that fosters mutual respect, recognizes the dignity and worth of all people, and promotes to the fullest, equal employment opportunity through affirmative action.   In compliance with the Clery Act of 1998, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville  Annual Security & Annual Fire Safety Report  is available for review. Call the UW-Platteville Campus Police Office at 608.342.1584 for a paper copy of the annual report. The Universities of Wisconsin will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released.  See Wis. Stat. §. 19.36(7). UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. Required Postings: Labor Law Poster - English Labor Law Poster - Spanish Families First Coronavirus Response Act Update"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Sociology_JR10015747": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Sociology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor of Sociology Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Position Summary: The Department of History and Social Sciences at the University of Wisconsin-Platteville is seeking a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Sociology with an anticipated start date of August 2027. This is a nine-month, benefit-eligible position with a salary range of $58,000-$62,000 per academic year, commensurate with qualifications and experience. The University of Wisconsin-Platteville is a comprehensive four-year institution dedicated to empowering professional and personal growth to prepare students to be critical thinkers and lifelong learners. The Sociology program strives to educate students to have knowledge and appreciation of sociology, and to sustainably improve their understanding of themselves and the critical structures and forces affecting society. Key Job Responsibilities: We encourage applicants with teaching and research experience in sociology who can teach courses in Introduction to Sociology, Social Inequalities, and other electives related to the candidate's area of expertise. The area of expertise is open; however, we are particularly interested in candidates with expertise in the areas of Rural/Urban Sociology & Rurality, Public Health Policy, or Environmental Sociology. A focus on an applied sociological approach and the ability to teach Social Research Methods are a plus. The successful candidate will teach four courses per semester (4/4), including introductory and upper-division sociology courses offered by the Department of History and Social Sciences. Applicants will also be expected to participate in scholarly activities (such as research, publishing, and presenting at conferences), and engage in service to the discipline, department, college, and the University. Additional teaching or other responsibilities may be available over the summer/winterim depending on program, college, or university needs and the interest of the selected candidate Other responsibilities include: Employing evidence-informed pedagogy practices to teach and engage undergraduate students within and beyond the classroom. Collaborating with students and faculty on academic, research, and program-related activities. Academic advising. Contribute to curriculum development and assessment reports. Other duties as assigned by the Department Chair. Required Qualifications: A PhD in sociology or closely related field earned by January 1, 2028. Advanced knowledge and skills related to sociology documented by a record of professional development, research, or publications. Minimum of one semester (4.5 months) experience teaching sociology at the undergraduate level. A record of research or a research agenda in the candidate’s area of interest within the discipline. Minimum of one semester (4.5 months) mentoring, advising, and/or leading undergraduate student organizations. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Why it is great to be a Pioneer: The University of Wisconsin-Platteville, founded in 1866, offers associate, baccalaureate, and master’s degree programs in a broad spectrum of disciplines including: science, technology, engineering, and mathematics; criminal justice; education; business; agriculture; and liberal arts. The Platteville campus is located in Southwest Wisconsin's largest community, which has a rich history rooted in mining —particularly lead mining —dating back to the early 19th century. The region offers excellent school systems, high-quality medical and hospital facilities, outstanding recreational opportunities, and vibrant businesses and industries. UW-Platteville serves as a cultural and educational center for the Tri-State region of Illinois, Iowa, and Wisconsin. At the University of Wisconsin-Platteville, the Department of History and Social Sciences offers a rich, interdisciplinary education that empowers students to explore the forces shaping human experience—past and present. Programs within this department include: ethnic studies, history, international studies, political science, social studies education, sociology, women’s and gender studies, pre-law, and cultural competence. Faculty and staff in the Department of History and Social Sciences equip their students through teaching, research, and service. Application Deadline: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by November 7, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position has been filled. The following documents are required for applicant consideration: Letter of application addressing all required qualifications. A curriculum vitae. A statement of teaching philosophy. Sample syllabi (introductory and upper division sociology courses). Contact information (name, telephone, and email address) for three professional references, at least two of which should address the candidate’s teaching. A separate statement describing a history of working with (or demonstrated commitment to working with) undergraduate populations, including first-generation students. For questions regarding this position, please contact the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Shan Sappleton, at sappletons@uwplatt.edu. Legal Notices and Important Information:  Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you and your references to answer questions regarding sexual violence and sexual harassment.   The University ensures physical accessibility to work environments for persons with disabilities and will provided reasonable accommodations to ensure equal access to employment. Upon request, the University will provide reasonable accommodations for religious observances and practices.   The University is committed to a program of affirmative action for women, racial minorities, persons with disabilities, disabled veterans and veterans of the Vietnam era. While the Chancellor assumes overall responsibility for the success of the program, university administrators and supervisors are responsible and accountable for implementation. Authority for monitoring the program is delegated to Human Resources.   Each individual associated with the University is called upon to pledge a new and revitalized commitment to build and maintain a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination, an environment that fosters mutual respect, recognizes the dignity and worth of all people, and promotes to the fullest, equal employment opportunity through affirmative action.   In compliance with the Clery Act of 1998, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville  Annual Security & Annual Fire Safety Report  is available for review. Call the UW-Platteville Campus Police Office at 608.342.1584 for a paper copy of the annual report. The Universities of Wisconsin will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released.  See Wis. Stat. §. 19.36(7). UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. Required Postings: Labor Law Poster - English Labor Law Poster - Spanish Families First Coronavirus Response Act Update"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Aerospace-Engineering_JR10014875": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Mechanical Engineering - Aerospace",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor Mechanical Engineering - Aerospace Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Position Summary: The Mechanical and Industrial Engineering department seeks a new full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor Mechanical Engineering - Aerospace . Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate engineering courses and labs, engaging in professional development activities, and providing services to the department and the university. The department is seeking candidates with the ability to teach in at least one core area of mechanical engineering. In addition, special consideration will be given to candidates with demonstrated ability and interest to develop and teach aerospace-focused courses in any combination of areas, including but not limited to Aerodynamics, Aerospace Propulsion, Flight Dynamics and Control, Space Systems, and Aerospace Structures. Please see the curriculum as listed in the catalog ( catalog ). This is a full-time, benefit-eligible position with a 9-month academic appointment starting fall 2027. The salary range for this position is $76,000 - 80,000, commensurate with experience.The Universities of Wisconsin has a nationally top ranked retirement plan ( plan URL ) and a comprehensive benefits package ( package URL ). Relocation assistance and Trailing Partners Engagement Program are available.  Key Job Responsibilities: The primary duty will be teaching undergraduate courses in Mechanical Engineering in different modalities; additionally, this position will require the creation of two or more technical elective courses that may lead to the creation of an aerospace engineering emphasis area or minor. Mechanical engineering faculty members are expected to participate in curriculum development, academic assessment, and professional development which among other things may include undergraduate research and scholarly activities, as well as university/community service.  Mechanical engineering faculty serve as academic advisors and are expected to work with colleagues across campus in cross-disciplinary collaborations outside the classroom. Additionally, faculty may have the opportunity to teach graduate level courses as part of our online MS Engineering program. Required Qualifications: To be considered for this position, applicants must respond to all required qualifications. PhD in Mechanical Engineering, Aerospace Engineering or a closely related field, earned by the start date of the position. Ability to utilize pedagogies that are proven to increase students’ engagement and retention. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Record of professional development, or willingness to develop in areas related to teaching, research, or scholarly work. Ability and interest in teaching/developing courses in aerospace related systems. About Mechanical and Industrial Engineering at University of Wisconsin-Platteville: Mechanical Engineering is the largest major at UW-Platteville. It currently has 19 tenured/tenure-track faculty and 7 instructional academic staff serving approximately 1000 students and graduating about 190 annually. The program is primarily based on the Platteville campus, with an additional 60-100 students enrolled through the online, asynchronous Platteville Engineering Partnership (PEP) Program. Our department offers a hands-on, experiential education, and our graduates are highly sought after across Wisconsin, Iowa, Illinois, and beyond. We maintain strong, long-standing partnerships with the industry throughout the region. The program is housed in a new state-of-the-art engineering facility. The Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Department emphasizes interdisciplinary coursework, cross-disciplinary collaboration, and modern inclusive pedagogy. We are a student-centered program committed to accessible undergraduate education, effective teaching practices, and fostering an inclusive learning environment. Anticipated starting salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. This position is benefit-eligible ( benefit URL ). About the College of Engineering, Mathematics and Science: The Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Department is in the College of Engineering, Mathematics, and Science (EMS). The College of EMS serves about 2,500 undergraduate students and 70 graduate students. The College of EMS’s values are provided below: We, the employees of the College of EMS, value being a key player in our undergraduate students’ success: We value teaching excellence, continuously improving our teaching skills, creating engaging learning environments, and implementing known best practices in STEM education We value nurturing a culture of care toward our students and our colleagues We value the university’s goal of serving students with a wide range of preparation, and we meet our students where they are We value high standards to prepare our students for the rigors of the STEM workplace We value providing job-ready skills to our students through authentic, applied, and hands-on learning experiences Why It’s Great to be a Pioneer: The University of Wisconsin-Platteville ( UW-Platteville URL ), founded in 1866, offers 47 baccalaureate and 10 master's programs. It possesses institutional strengths in middle level education, engineering, industrial technology, agriculture, criminal justice, and business. The campus is located in Southwest Wisconsin's largest and most historic community. The region offers excellent school systems, high quality medical and hospital facilities, outstanding recreational opportunities, and vibrant businesses and industries. UW-Platteville is a cultural and educational center for the Tri-State region of Illinois, Iowa and Wisconsin. Take a virtual tour of our campus! (t our URL ) Application Deadline: Applications must be submitted through our online applicant portal ( application portal URL ). Priority will be given to applications received by January 18th, but will remain open until the position has been filled. How to Apply: The following documents are required for applicant consideration: Letter of application addressing the position summary and all required qualifications A statement of teaching philosophy A current curriculum vitae Unofficial transcript for doctoral degree Letters of support and contact information from three professional references; we also reserve the right to contact these individuals QUESTIONS MAY BE ADDRESSED TO: Dr. John Obielodan Chair, Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Email: obielodanj@uwplatt.edu Legal Notices and Important Information:  Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you and your references to answer questions regarding sexual violence and sexual harassment.   The University ensures physical accessibility to work environments for persons with disabilities and will provided reasonable accommodations to ensure equal access to employment. Upon request, the University will provide reasonable accommodations for religious observances and practices.   The University is committed to a program of affirmative action for women, racial minorities, persons with disabilities, disabled veterans and veterans of the Vietnam era. While the Chancellor assumes overall responsibility for the success of the program, university administrators and supervisors are responsible and accountable for implementation. Authority for monitoring the program is delegated to Human Resources.   Each individual associated with the University is called upon to pledge a new and revitalized commitment to build and maintain a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination, an environment that fosters mutual respect, recognizes the dignity and worth of all people, and promotes to the fullest, equal employment opportunity through affirmative action.   In compliance with the Clery Act of 1998, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville  Annual Security & Annual Fire Safety Report  is available for review. Call the UW-Platteville Campus Police Office at 608.342.1584 for a paper copy of the annual report. The Universities of Wisconsin will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released.  See Wis. Stat. §. 19.36(7). UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. Required Postings: Labor Law Poster - English Labor Law Poster - Spanish Families First Coronavirus Response Act Update"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Special-Education_JR10015350": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Special Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor of Special Education Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Position Summary: The School of Education seeks two full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professors of Special Education with an anticipated start date of August 2027. The successful candidates will teach undergraduate courses in special education and professional education, engage in research and professional activity, and contribute to university and departmental service. Candidates with experience teaching in rural settings are especially encouraged to apply, in alignment with the School of Education’s mission to meet the needs of rural communities. The Universities of Wisconsin has a nationally top ranked retirement plan  and a comprehensive benefits package. Salary for this position is $60,000-$64,000 per academic year, commensurate with qualifications and experience. Key Job Responsibilities: The primary responsibility will be teaching a variety of undergraduate courses, both in person and online, including courses within the special education minor, the introduction to special education course for all education majors, and professional education courses. Supervising student teaching and pre-student teaching clinical experiences may also be included. The typical teaching load is 12 credits per semester. Additional duties Include : Utilize current best practices in teacher education to support undergraduate student learning in the classroom. Mentor and advise undergraduate students including undergraduate student research and scholarly activities. Provide leadership for the Special Education Minor. Engage in departmental, university, and community service and outreach activities including working with PreK-12 educators. Collaborate with faculty and staff colleagues within the School of Education, across campus, and in partner K-12 schools to complete program tasks and goals. Engage in research or scholarly activity and continuing education. Assess student learning outcomes and engage in feedback-driven course and program improvements. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. or Ed.D. in special education, curriculum and instruction, counseling, educational leadership, or closely related field. Degree must be conferred by January 1, 2028. Demonstrated professional experience applying inclusive, evidence-based practices and applicable legal requirements to design, implement, and evaluate PreK–12 learning environments to support students with special needs. Demonstrated professional experience collaborating with stakeholders such as families, general education teachers, pupil services professionals, or related service providers to support learning for students with special needs. Evidence of a strong commitment to undergraduate education including the preparation of pre-service teachers. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Advanced knowledge and skills related to special education documented by a record of professional development, research, or publications related to special education. Why it is great to be a Pioneer: The University of Wisconsin-Platteville, founded in 1866, offers associate, baccalaureate, and master’s degree programs in a broad spectrum of disciplines including: science, technology, engineering, and mathematics; criminal justice; education; business; agriculture; and liberal arts. The Platteville campus is located in Southwest Wisconsin's largest community, which has a rich history rooted in mining —particularly lead mining —dating back to the early 19th century. The region offers excellent school systems, high-quality medical and hospital facilities, outstanding recreational opportunities, and vibrant businesses and industries. UW-Platteville serves as a cultural and educational center for the Tri-State region of Illinois, Iowa, and Wisconsin. The School of Education in the College of Liberal Arts and Education serves as the academic home for the Elementary Education major and a growing Special Education minor leading to K-12 licensure. The school also supports all campus programs leading to teacher licensure. The school models a place-based framework by partnering with school districts and other resources in the rural communities of the region. Application Deadline: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by October 24, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position has been filled. The following documents are required for applicant consideration: Letter of application addressing all required qualifications Current curriculum vitae Contact information (name, telephone number, and email address) for three professional references For questions regarding this position, please contact the School of Education at education@uwplatt.edu. Legal Notices and Important Information:  Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you and your references to answer questions regarding sexual violence and sexual harassment.   The University ensures physical accessibility to work environments for persons with disabilities and will provided reasonable accommodations to ensure equal access to employment. Upon request, the University will provide reasonable accommodations for religious observances and practices.   The University is committed to a program of affirmative action for women, racial minorities, persons with disabilities, disabled veterans and veterans of the Vietnam era. While the Chancellor assumes overall responsibility for the success of the program, university administrators and supervisors are responsible and accountable for implementation. Authority for monitoring the program is delegated to Human Resources.   Each individual associated with the University is called upon to pledge a new and revitalized commitment to build and maintain a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination, an environment that fosters mutual respect, recognizes the dignity and worth of all people, and promotes to the fullest, equal employment opportunity through affirmative action.   In compliance with the Clery Act of 1998, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville  Annual Security & Annual Fire Safety Report  is available for review. Call the UW-Platteville Campus Police Office at 608.342.1584 for a paper copy of the annual report. The Universities of Wisconsin will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released.  See Wis. Stat. §. 19.36(7). UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. Required Postings: Labor Law Poster - English Labor Law Poster - Spanish Families First Coronavirus Response Act Update"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-Mechanical-and-Industrial-Engineering-Notice-of-Filing_JR10015241": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Notice of Filing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\n\nNOTICE OF FILING - Please do not apply to this position as it has been filled. This posting is mandatory to meet a United States Department of Labor requirement. See the job posting for more details. A competitive recruitment and selection process was conducted for this employment opportunity and a U.S. worker was not selected. An application for Alien Employment Certification is being filed on behalf of an alien to fill the employment opportunity. Anyone with documentary evidence relative to the application, or available workers, wages and/or working conditions, may contact the Regional Certifying Office of the Department of Labor at the following address:  U.S. Department of Labor Employment and Training Administration Office of Foreign Labor Certification 200 Constitution Avenue NW, Room N– 5311 Washington, DC 20210  Institution: University of Wisconsin Platteville Position Title: Assistant Professor Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Notice of Filing Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Job Duties: The Mechanical and Industrial Engineering department seeks multiple new full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professors in Mechanical Engineering. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate engineering courses and labs, engaging in professional development activities, and providing services to the department and the university. The department is seeking candidates with the desire and ability to teach a broad range of mechanical engineering   courses. Primary consideration will be given to candidates with a willingness to develop courses in emerging areas of Manufacturing and to teach Manufacturing Processes, Engineering Materials, and Design of Machine Elements.  Secondary consideration will be given to those who can also teach Engineering Modeling & Design and Mechanisms and Machines. Candidates with strong backgrounds in other areas may be considered.  Please see the curriculum as listed in the  catalog . This is a full-time, benefit-eligible position with a 9-month academic appointment. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The Universities of Wisconsin System has a  nationally top ranked retirement plan  and a  comprehensive benefits package . Relocation assistance and Trailing Partners Engagement Program are available. Key Job Responsibilities: The primary duty will be teaching undergraduate courses in Mechanical Engineering in different modalities.  Mechanical engineering faculty members are expected to participate in curriculum development, academic assessment, and professional development which among other things may include undergraduate research and scholarly activities, as well as university/community service. Mechanical engineering faculty serve as academic advisors and are expected to work with colleagues across campus in cross-disciplinary collaborations outside the classroom. Additionally, faculty may have the opportunity to teach graduate level courses as part of our online MS Engineering program. Required Qualifications: To be considered for this position, applicants must respond to all required qualifications. PhD in Mechanical or Industrial Engineering, Engineering Mechanics, Aerospace Engineering, Biomedical Engineering, or a closely related field, must earned by start date of position. Ability to utilize pedagogies that are proven to increase students’ engagement and retention. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Record of professional development, or willingness to develop in areas related to teaching, research, or scholarly work. About Mechanical and Industrial Engineering at University of Wisconsin-Platteville Mechanical Engineering is the largest major at UW-Platteville. It currently has 20 tenured/tenure-track faculty and 7 instructional academic staff serving approximately 1000 students and graduating about 190 annually. The program is primarily based on the Platteville campus, with an additional 60-100 students enrolled through the online, asynchronous Platteville Engineering Partnership (PEP) Program. Our department offers a hands-on, experiential education, and our graduates are highly sought after across Wisconsin, Iowa, Illinois, and beyond. We maintain strong, long-standing partnerships with the industry throughout the region. The program is housed in a new state-of-the-art engineering facility. The Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Department emphasizes interdisciplinary coursework, cross-disciplinary collaboration, and modern inclusive pedagogy. We are a student-centered program committed to accessible undergraduate education, effective teaching practices, and fostering an inclusive learning environment. Anticipated starting salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience.  This position is  benefit -eligible. About the College of Engineering, Mathematics and Science: The Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Department is in the College of Engineering, Mathematics, and Science (EMS). The College of EMS serves about 2,500 undergraduate students and 70 graduate students. The College of EMS’s values are provided below: We, the employees of the College of EMS, value being a key player in our undergraduate students’ success: We value teaching excellence, continuously improving our teaching skills, creating engaging learning environments, and implementing known best practices in STEM education We value nurturing a culture of care toward our students and our colleagues We value the university’s goal of serving students with a wide range of preparation, and we meet our students where they are We value high standards to prepare our students for the rigors of the STEM workplace We value providing job-ready skills to our students through authentic, applied, and hands-on learning experiences Why It’s Great to be a Pioneer: The  University of Wisconsin-Platteville , founded in 1866, offers 47 baccalaureate and 10 master's programs. It possesses institutional strengths in middle level education, engineering, industrial technology, agriculture, criminal justice, and business. The campus is located in Southwest Wisconsin's largest and most historic community. The region offers excellent school systems, high quality medical and hospital facilities, outstanding recreational opportunities, and vibrant businesses and industries. UW-Platteville is a cultural and educational center for the Tri-State region of Illinois, Iowa and Wisconsin. Take a virtual tour of our campus! Application Deadline: Applications must be submitted through our  online applicant portal . Applications will be accepted until the position has been filled. How to Apply: The following documents are required for applicant consideration: Letter of application addressing the position summary and all required qualifications A statement of teaching philosophy A current curriculum vitae Contact information (name, telephone number, and email address) for 3 professional references. QUESTIONS MAY BE ADDRESSED TO: Dr. John Obielodan, Chair, Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering Email: obielodanj@uwplatt.edu"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Forensic-Investigation_JR10015160": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Forensic Investigation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor of Forensic Investigation Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Position Summary: The Department of Criminal Justice & Forensic Investigation at the University of Wisconsin-Platteville invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Forensic Investigation , a FEPAC accredited program. This is a full-time, nine-month, benefits eligible position starting in August of 2027, with a salary range of $64,000 - $68,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience. The ideal candidate will specialize in crime scene investigation and have relevant work experience; however, candidates of all specialties are encouraged to apply and will be considered. Key Job Responsibilities: The successful candidate will have a demonstrated competency teaching multiple existing courses (e.g., Fingerprinting & Other Biometrics, Crime Scene Processing II, Senior Seminar) and the capacity to develop and teach courses within their area of expertise. The typical teaching load is 4 courses per semester (4/4). Other responsibilities include: Engaging in scholarly activities. Providing academic advising to students. Participating in department, college, university and community service and outreach activities. Contributing to curriculum development, assessment, and revisions. Collaborating with students and faculty on academic, research, and program-related activities. Other duties may be assigned by the Department Chair. Required Qualifications: A doctoral degree in Forensic Science or related field earned by January 1, 2028 OR A masters of science (MS) in Forensic Science or a related field and at least five years of professional work experience in forensics. Experience teaching at least one semester of an undergraduate-level course. Evidence of a strong commitment to undergraduate education in a liberal arts environment.  Excellent oral and written communication skills.  Why it is great to be a Pioneer: The University of Wisconsin-Platteville, founded in 1866, offers associate, baccalaureate, and master’s degree programs in a broad spectrum of disciplines including: science, technology, engineering, and mathematics; criminal justice; education; business; agriculture; and liberal arts. The Platteville campus is located in Southwest Wisconsin's largest community, which has a rich history rooted in mining —particularly lead mining —dating back to the early 19th century. The region offers excellent school systems, high-quality medical and hospital facilities, outstanding recreational opportunities, and vibrant businesses and industries. UW-Platteville serves as a cultural and educational center for the Tri-State region of Illinois, Iowa, and Wisconsin. The Department of Criminal Justice & Forensic Investigation’s Criminal Justice and Forensic Investigation programs in the College of Liberal Arts and Education have 8 full-time tenure-track positions and regularly employ an additional 8 instructional academic staff. The undergraduate program offers a BA/BS in Criminal Justice and BS in Forensic Investigation. The department offers a BS and MS in Criminal Justice in an online asynchronous modality. The department’s undergraduate majors combined have over 525 on-campus students and over 140 online students, making it the third largest department by undergraduate majors. In March 2026, the department received FEPAC accreditation for all three of the Forensic Investigation degree emphases (Crime Scene, Medicolegal Death Investigation, and Laboratory). This recognition also places UW-Platteville among just 32 bachelor's degree programs in the country to hold FEPAC accreditation. Five of those programs are accredited in Crime Scene Investigation. UW-Platteville is the only program in the country with an accredited Medicolegal Death Investigation emphasis.  The Forensic Investigation program is home to the Forensic Investigation Crime Scene House (FICSH), the Decomposition/Taphonomy facility adjoining the FICSH, a criminalistics/fingerprinting lab, and a dry forensic anthropology lab. With around 340 students, the Forensic Investigation program is the fifth largest on-campus major. Application Deadline: To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by October 17, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position has been filled. The following documents are required for applicant consideration: Cover letter addressing all required qualifications. The letter should explicitly state how the applicant’s education, experience, and/or area(s) of expertise would contribute to and/or enhance the department’s existing strengths.   A Curriculum Vitae; Contact information (name, telephone number, and email address) for three professional references; A statement of your teaching philosophy. For questions regarding this position, please contact Dr. Janamarie Truesdell at truesdellj@uwplatt.edu. Legal Notices and Important Information:  Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you and your references to answer questions regarding sexual violence and sexual harassment.   The University ensures physical accessibility to work environments for persons with disabilities and will provided reasonable accommodations to ensure equal access to employment. Upon request, the University will provide reasonable accommodations for religious observances and practices.   The University is committed to a program of affirmative action for women, racial minorities, persons with disabilities, disabled veterans and veterans of the Vietnam era. While the Chancellor assumes overall responsibility for the success of the program, university administrators and supervisors are responsible and accountable for implementation. Authority for monitoring the program is delegated to Human Resources.   Each individual associated with the University is called upon to pledge a new and revitalized commitment to build and maintain a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination, an environment that fosters mutual respect, recognizes the dignity and worth of all people, and promotes to the fullest, equal employment opportunity through affirmative action.   In compliance with the Clery Act of 1998, the University of Wisconsin-Platteville  Annual Security & Annual Fire Safety Report  is available for review. Call the UW-Platteville Campus Police Office at 608.342.1584 for a paper copy of the annual report. The Universities of Wisconsin will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released.  See Wis. Stat. §. 19.36(7). UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. Required Postings: Labor Law Poster - English Labor Law Poster - Spanish Families First Coronavirus Response Act Update"
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Platteville-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Forensic-Investigation_JR10015055": {
